insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-elbow', 1, 'The elbow on the 180 cored rows (GR, RHOB, NPHI, PEF, standard scaling, seed 3, 10 starts) begins at k 1. What inertia does that first row print?', '["4.000000, one unit for each standardised log, since the one cluster sits at the mean of every log", "720.000000, the number of rows times the number of logs", "245.390847, because a single cluster already absorbs the first drop the curve can show", "Nothing at all, because inertia needs at least two centres before a sum of squares exists"]'::jsonb, 1, 'At k 1 every row sits in one cluster about the mean, and each standardised log contributes n = 180 to the sum of squares, so four logs give 720.000000. 4.000000 is what the correlation eigenvalues sum to, a different quantity. 245.390847 is the inertia at k 2, and the elbow prints a k 1 row, with no drop beside it.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-elbow', 2, 'At k 4 the elbow prints a drop of 22.942083. What does it print as the drop fraction at k 4?', '["0.668972, the k 4 drop set against the inertia of the k 2 row above it", "22.942083 again, since the drop and its fraction are one column printed twice", "0.112303, since each fraction is read on the row after the drop it belongs to", "0.282430, the drop divided by the inertia at k 3"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis reads "inertia(k - 1) - inertia(k); dropFraction divides by inertia(k - 1)", so the k 4 fraction is 22.942083 over the k 3 inertia 81.231125, which prints 0.282430. 0.668972 is the fraction at k 3 and 0.112303 the fraction at k 5; the drop and the fraction are separate columns.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-elbow', 3, 'Over k 2 to 8, at which k do the largest drop and the largest drop fraction fall?', '["The drop peaks at k 2, 474.609153, and the fraction at k 3, 0.668972", "At k 2 for both: drop 474.609153, fraction 0.659179", "Both peak at k 3: drop 164.159721, fraction 0.668972", "k 4 for the drop, 22.942083, where the core''s four facies sit, and k 5 for the fraction"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The largest drop is 474.609153 at k 2, taken from the one-cluster figure 720.000000. The fraction at k 2 is 0.659179, a little below the 0.668972 at k 3, and the drop at k 3 is 164.159721, well below the k 2 drop. The k 4 drop, 22.942083, is smaller than both.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-elbow', 4, 'An elbow is run with withSilhouette true. Which k does it return as its reading of the inertia curve?', '["The k with the largest drop fraction, k 3, returned as the elbow", "k 4, returned as the elbow because the core describes 4 facies", "None; it prints the drops, and the one k it names is bestSilhouetteK", "Whichever k has the smallest inertia: k 8, at 41.863457"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis reads "bestSilhouetteK has the highest mean silhouette; a tie goes to the smaller k. No elbow is picked automatically: read the drops". The elbow is never told the core''s four facies, and the smallest inertia is no answer because inertia keeps falling as k grows.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-elbow', 5, 'With one start per k and seed 265 over k 1 to 8, the k 8 inertia is 45.270437 against 45.153181 at k 7. What does the engine do?', '["Refuses the call and names nInit, since the curve rises", "It returns the result, lists k 8 in inertiaRises and adds a warning", "Swaps the k 7 centres in at k 8 to keep the curve falling", "Quietly raises nInit to 10 and prints 41.697291 at k 8 with no message"]'::jsonb, 1, 'An inertia rise is a warning and never a refusal: the engine lists the k in `inertiaRises` and warns, in its own words, "inertia rises at k = 8: those runs stopped in a local minimum; raise nInit". It changes no run; 41.697291 is what the same seed gives when the caller asks for the default 10 starts.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-elbow', 6, 'The same seed 265 elbow is rerun with the default 10 starts at every k. What does it print at k 8?', '["45.270437, the one-start figure, since the seed fixes the first start", "41.863457, the seed 3 figure, since ten starts at every seed reach one value", "45.153181, the k 7 figure carried up so the rise is removed", "41.697291, and no rise is flagged"]'::jsonb, 3, 'With the default 10 starts the seed 265 elbow shows no rise, reading 41.697291 at k 8. 45.270437 and 45.153181 are the one-start figures at k 8 and k 7, and 41.863457 is the seed 3 elbow at k 8: two seeds with ten starts need not agree.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-elbow', 7, 'An elbow at seed 3 with 10 starts prints 47.642067 at k 6. What does a single kmeans call at k 6, seed 3, 10 starts return?', '["A different inertia, since k 1 to 5 used up the stream first", "44.956011, the run started for the k above it", "47.642067, because each k runs its own fresh mulberry32(seed) stream", "No inertia until nInit is given, since a lone kmeans call has no default starts"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis reads "kmeans(X, k, seed, nInit) for each k, each k with its own mulberry32(seed) stream, so a row equals the single kmeans call", and at k 6 both return 47.642067. 44.956011 is the k 7 inertia, and kmeans takes 10 starts when nInit is left out.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-elbow', 8, 'At k 5 the elbow prints 11 passes for the winning start. What do those passes count?', '["Assignment passes of the winning start, the confirming pass included", "Centre updates summed over all 10 starts at k 5, one for each move of a centre", "Starts at k 5 whose inertia came out within reach of the winner", "Sweeps needed before the drop fraction at k 5 stopped changing"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The k-means basis reads "iterations counts assignment passes (scikit-learn n_iter_)", the pass that confirms the labels included, and the elbow prints them for the winning start only. The count describes one start, and the drop fraction is computed once from two finished runs.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-elbow', 9, 'An elbow is asked for with kMin 0. What comes back?', '["A run from k 1, since the engine treats a kMin of 0 as the default lower end", "An extra row at k 0 with an inertia of 0, since no cluster leaves nothing to sum", "The kMax refusal, since kMax must lie from kMin to 180", "A refusal naming kMin: \"kMin must be a whole number, 1 or more\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine refuses a kMin of 0 by name, in its own words "kMin must be a whole number, 1 or more". It never substitutes a default and never prints a k 0 row; the kMax message is a separate refusal, raised when kMax falls below kMin.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-elbow', 10, 'An elbow asks for kMin 4 and a kMax below it. Which field does the refusal name, and what range does it state?', '["kMin, from 1 up to kMax, since the smaller end of the range is the one out of place", "kMax, from kMin (4) to 180 (the number of rows)", "k, from 1 to 180, the kmeans range, because the elbow passes each k to kmeans", "nInit, since a range running backwards leaves no starts to draw"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s own words are "kMax must be a whole number from kMin (4) to 180 (the number of rows)". It names kMax and states both ends: kMin at the bottom and the number of rows at the top.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-elbow', 11, 'An elbow call passes a word where withSilhouette belongs. What does the engine do?', '["Refuses it: \"withSilhouette must be true or false\"", "Scores the silhouette anyway, since any word that is not empty counts as true", "Skips the silhouette, as if it were false", "Returns the elbow with a warning that the silhouette setting was read as false"]'::jsonb, 0, 'withSilhouette must be a true or false value, and a word is refused by name in the engine''s words "withSilhouette must be true or false". The engine neither coerces the word nor falls back to a default with a warning.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-elbow', 12, 'Read down the fraction column of the seed 3 elbow. Which description holds?', '["Falls at every step after k 2, so the curve bends only once and smoothly down to k 8", "Its lowest figure is the last one, 0.068791 at k 8, as the curve flattens out toward the end", "It falls to 0.282430 at k 4 and 0.112303 at k 5, and no larger k shown reaches 0.112303 again", "Rises from 0.282430 at k 4 to 0.112303 at k 5, which makes k 4 a dip in the column"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The fractions read 0.659179, 0.668972, 0.282430, 0.112303, 0.079256, 0.056380 and 0.068791 for k 2 to 8. They rise from k 2 to k 3 and again from k 7 to k 8, so the fall is not smooth; the lowest is 0.056380 at k 7; and 0.112303 is below 0.282430.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-elbow', 13, 'Why is the k with the smallest inertia no answer to the choice of k?', '["The smallest inertia sits at k 1, where every row shares the one centre at the mean", "Inertia falls as k grows when every k is fitted well, down to 0 at one cluster per distinct row", "Inertia is in squared standard units, and those units change from one k to the next", "Past the number of facies, extra centres make the inertia rise again with k"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course states that inertia always falls as k grows when every k is fitted well, reaching 0 at one cluster per distinct row. k 1 carries the largest inertia, 720.000000; the units are the same standard units at every k; and on these rows the inertia falls at every step from k 1 to k 8, ending at 41.863457.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-elbow', 14, 'At k 4 with seeds 1 to 10, how do one start and ten starts compare against the lowest inertia 58.289042?', '["8 of the 10 seeds stop above it with one start; with ten, 9 of the 10 reach it", "5 of the 10 seeds stop above it with one start; with ten starts all 10 of them reach it", "8 of the 10 seeds stop above it with one start; with ten starts all 10 of them reach it", "2 of the 10 stop above it with one start; ten starts change none"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With one start, 8 of the 10 seeds stop above 58.289042, as high as 78.775612. With ten starts 9 of the 10 reach it and seed 5 stops at 58.297079, so ten starts make a poor stop less likely without ruling it out.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-elbow', 15, 'Why is an elbow quoted with its scaling?', '["Min-max scaling reaches 4.258929 at k 4, a tighter fit than the 58.289042 of standard scaling", "Scaling changes only the passes each start takes, and the inertia comes back the same", "Inertia is in squared units of the scaled logs: at k 4 standard reads 58.289042 and raw 10331.820703", "The raw inertia is the standard inertia put back in log units, so both describe one fit"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The three scalings at k 4, seed 3, 10 starts give 58.289042 standard, 10331.820703 raw and 4.258929 min-max. These are in different units and cannot be compared, so 4.258929 is no tighter fit, and the raw figure is a different clustering on different distances.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-silhouette', 1, 'Four stated points, 0, 1, 4 and 6, are labelled 0, 0, 1, 1 with no scaling. For the point at 0, what are a, b and its silhouette?', '["a 1.000000, b 4.000000 from the nearest point of the other cluster, silhouette 0.750000", "a 0.000000 as the point is its own nearest row, b 5.000000, silhouette 1.000000", "a 1.000000, b 5.000000, silhouette 0.800000", "Its own-cluster distance 1.000000 and b 5.000000 give b less a, 4.000000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'a is the mean distance to the other rows of its own cluster, here the point at 1, so 1.000000; b is the MEAN distance to the other cluster, (4 + 6) / 2 = 5.000000; and s = (b - a) / max(a, b) = 0.800000, which the engine returns. Taking the nearest or the farthest point for b, or leaving out the division by max(a, b), is another formula.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-silhouette', 2, 'Of those four points, which scores the lowest silhouette, and why?', '["The point at 4, 0.428571: it sits 2 from its partner at 6 and only 3 from the point at 1", "The point at 6, 0.636364, since it is the farthest point from the other cluster", "At 1, 0.750000, because the point at 1 lies nearest the other cluster''s points", "None of them; all four score 0.653734, the mean the engine returns"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine returns 0.800000, 0.750000, 0.428571 and 0.636364 for the points at 0, 1, 4 and 6, with a mean of 0.653734. The point at 4 is lowest because its own cluster is barely tighter than the other one. The mean is one summary, and the rows score differently.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-silhouette', 3, 'Which cluster of the teaching clustering has the lowest mean silhouette?', '["Cluster 1, 0.438615, which is also the largest cluster at 59 rows", "Every cluster reads 0.545063, since the mean is shared by the clusters", "Cluster 2 at 0.842778, the cluster whose centre sits farthest out", "Cluster 3, 0.245762, a cluster of 38 rows"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The cluster means are 0.599453, 0.438615, 0.842778 and 0.245762 for clusters 0 to 3, and 0.545063 over all 180 rows. Cluster 3 is lowest; cluster 2 is the highest, and 0.545063 is the overall mean blending all four.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-silhouette', 4, 'Scored in the same standardised space, how does the mean silhouette of the core facies as labels compare with the k-means clusters, and what does that show?', '["0.528711 against 0.545063, which shows the k-means clusters match the rock better than the core itself does", "0.528711 against 0.545063; the silhouette scores compact, separate groups and is silent on rock types", "Level at 0.842345 against 0.842778, so the two labellings are equally good descriptions of the rock", "Far lower at 0.152252 against 0.245762, which shows k-means has repaired the shaly-sand facies"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The core facies score 0.528711 and the k-means clusters 0.545063. A silhouette scores how compact and apart groups are and says nothing of whether they are rock types, so a higher figure for k-means is no claim that it matches rock. 0.842345 and 0.842778 are the limestone and cluster 2 figures; 0.152252 and 0.245762 are shaly-sand and cluster 3.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-silhouette', 5, 'How many rows of the teaching clustering score below 0, and what do they share?', '["3 rows, each core shaly-sand in cluster 3, from three different wells", "0 rows, since a k-means row sits nearest its own centre and so nearest its own cluster", "12, the rows scaling moved next to a row of their own facies", "Three rows of cluster 1, which holds sandstone and shaly-sand"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Rows 12, 135 and 169, of EKENE-1, EKENE-5 and EKENE-6, score -0.045137, -0.002304 and -0.021661; all are shaly-sand in cluster 3. The nearest centre is no promise of the nearest cluster on average distance, which is what b measures, so rows below 0 can exist. 12 is the count of rows scaling moved, a different table.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-silhouette', 6, 'What silhouette does the engine give a row alone in its cluster?', '["1, since a lone row is perfectly separated from every other cluster", "-1, since a row with no partner of its own is scored as misplaced", "0, the stated rule, following scikit-learn", "No value, since the call is refused naming labels"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis reads "a row alone in its cluster scores 0, and a = b = 0 scores 0 (scikit-learn)". The row has no a, and the stated score is 0. The call is not refused: the average-linkage cut at k 4 leaves row 56 alone and returns 0.000000 for it.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-silhouette', 7, 'The average-linkage cut at k 4 has clusters of 96, 54, 29 and 1 rows. What is its mean silhouette, and what does that mean hide?', '["0.000000, since a lone row in the labelling sets the mean silhouette of all of them to 0", "0.514177; on its own it shows neither the cluster of one row nor the cluster of 96", "0.545063, since two labellings at k 4 of these rows score the same", "0.653734, with a warning beside it naming the lone row"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The average-linkage cut scores 0.514177, a little below the k-means 0.545063, and the mean alone gives no hint of the lone row 56 or of the 96-row cluster; the sizes show them. The lone row contributes 0 and does not zero the mean. 0.653734 is the mean of the four stated points.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-silhouette', 8, 'The teaching k-means labels are scored on the raw logs with scale ''none''. What does the mean silhouette read?', '["0.545063 again, because the labels are the same and a label cannot move", "It reads 0.528711, the figure of the core facies in the same space", "Nothing: raw logs are refused for their units", "0.443238, since the silhouette is measured in a space"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The same labels read 0.443238 on the raw logs against 0.545063 on the standardised logs, so a silhouette is quoted with its scaling. 0.528711 is the core facies in the standardised space, and scale ''none'' is accepted.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-silhouette', 9, 'A 60-row sample of the teaching clustering scores 0.521590 at seed 3 and 0.525011 at seed 4. What follows?', '["Both samples round to the 180-row 0.545063", "Seed 4 is the better clustering, as it scores higher", "A sampled silhouette is quoted with its size and its seed", "They are the means of two clusters of 60 rows cut from the 180"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis reads "the first 60 rows of a mulberry32(3) Fisher-Yates shuffle, scored among themselves (scikit-learn sample_size)". The seed chooses the rows scored, so the figure moves with it, and all 180 rows score 0.545063. The clustering is the same at both seeds.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-silhouette', 10, 'A silhouette is asked for with every row in one cluster. What happens?', '["Refused: \"labels must hold from 2 to 179 distinct clusters (found 1): the silhouette compares each row with the next nearest cluster\"", "It returns 0 for every row, since a single cluster leaves no row with a b, and prints a mean of 0.000000 over all 180 rows", "Refused, naming X, because a single cluster gives the labels no variance for the standard scaler to divide by on the rows passed", "It returns each row''s distance to the one centre in place of b, since there is no other cluster for b to be measured against"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The silhouette needs a second cluster for b, and the engine refuses one cluster by name, in its own words quoted in the key. It does not score the rows 0, and the refusal names labels, the field it could not work with.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-silhouette', 11, 'Four rows are given four different labels. What does the engine do?', '["Scores each of the four rows 0, as rows alone in their clusters, for a mean of 0.000000", "A refusal naming labels, which on four rows accepts 2 to 3 distinct clusters (found 4)", "Merges the two nearest rows to make 3 clusters, then scores those three", "Scores every row 1.000000, since no row shares its cluster with another"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The accepted range runs from 2 to the number of rows less one; with every row alone no row has an a, and the engine refuses in its own words: "labels must hold from 2 to 3 distinct clusters (found 4): the silhouette compares each row with the next nearest cluster". The lone-row rule of 0 applies to a row alone inside an accepted labelling, and the engine never edits the labels it is given.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-silhouette', 12, 'A labels list holds numbers everywhere except a name at position 2. What does the engine say?', '["labels must be an array of 180 labels, one per row, as the list mixes types", "Converts the name to a number and scores the rows with the list as passed", "Nothing; it scores the rows, since a label is only a name for a group", "labels[2] must be the same type as labels[0]: all strings or all numbers"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s words are "labels[2] must be the same type as labels[0]: all strings or all numbers", naming the first entry of the other type. The one-per-row message is the refusal of a list of the wrong length, a separate check, and nothing is converted.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-silhouette', 13, 'Over k 2 to 8, seed 3, 10 starts, what does the elbow return as bestSilhouetteK?', '["4, the core''s facies count, where the mean silhouette is 0.545063", "2, at 0.650162, since a tie goes to the smaller k", "3, where the mean silhouette is 0.690362", "8, the k with the smallest inertia and the most clusters to score"]'::jsonb, 2, 'bestSilhouetteK has the highest mean silhouette, 0.690362 at k 3. The tie rule to the smaller k never comes into play, because 0.650162 at k 2 is lower; k 8 reads 0.266816, the lowest of the range.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-silhouette', 14, 'Which statement about the mean silhouette over k 2 to 8 on seed 3 matches the table?', '["It is highest at k 3, 0.690362, and reads 0.266816 at k 8", "Falling at every step from k 2 to k 8, it is highest at k 2", "Highest at k 4, 0.545063, the core''s count", "Once k passes 4 it rises at every step, up to 0.505098 at k 6"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The means read 0.650162, 0.690362, 0.545063, 0.503776, 0.505098, 0.492984 and 0.266816 for k 2 to 8. They rise from k 2 to k 3 and from k 5 to k 6, fall from k 6 to k 7, and peak at k 3.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-silhouette', 15, 'A row''s mean distance to the nearest other cluster, b, is below its mean distance to its own cluster, a. What is its silhouette?', '["Exactly 0, as for a row alone in its cluster, since a and b both exist", "Below 0: on average it sits nearer another cluster than its own", "Above 0 once divided by max(a, b), since that division makes it positive", "Undefined, since the engine refuses any row with b below a"]'::jsonb, 1, 's = (b - a) / max(a, b) is negative when b is below a, and the teaching clustering has 3 such rows, the lowest at -0.045137. Dividing by the positive max(a, b) keeps the sign, and nothing is refused.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-agglomerative-clustering', 1, 'Agglomerative clustering of the 180 cored rows keeps its whole merge history. How many rows does the linkage matrix hold?', '["180, one for each row of the data, the first row merging with itself", "179, one per merge, n - 1", "178, since the final merge into one cluster is left unrecorded", "Four, one per cluster of the cut the call was given"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The linkage matrix has one row per merge, n - 1 = 179 rows for 180 rows, and the last of them, merge 178, joins the final two clusters into all 180 rows. The cut at k is read from the history; it does not shorten it.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-agglomerative-clustering', 2, 'Why does an agglomerative result carry no seed, when a k-means result is quoted with one?', '["Its seed is fixed at 3 inside the engine, the teaching seed, so it need not be quoted", "It runs 10 starts of its own and keeps the lowest height, which settles any seed", "The seed is carried in the linkage matrix, in the size column of each merge", "The call takes no seed at all: its inputs are X, linkage, k, scale and names"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s function table lists agglomerative''s inputs as X, linkage, k, scale and names, with no seed and no starts; kmeans lists seed and nInit. Nothing in the merging is drawn at random, and the size column counts the data rows in each new cluster.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-agglomerative-clustering', 3, 'Which pair of clusters does Ward linkage merge at each step?', '["The pair whose union raises the within-cluster sum of squares least", "The pair whose two farthest rows are nearest each other", "The pair with the smallest mean distance over cross pairs", "Two clusters whose centres are nearest in raw log units"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ward merges the pair whose union raises the within-cluster sum of squares least, from Euclidean distances on the scaled logs. Judging by the two farthest rows is complete linkage, and by the mean over cross pairs is average linkage (UPGMA). The rows are standardised by default, so the distances are in standard units.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-agglomerative-clustering', 4, 'Cut at k 4, which linkage puts more rows into one cluster than any core facies holds?', '["Ward, whose cut holds a cluster of 54 rows, as many as the limestone count", "Complete linkage, with a largest cluster of 51 rows and three smaller ones", "Average linkage: 96 rows in one cluster, against 54 in the largest facies", "Every linkage alike, since each cut at k 4 has one cluster above 54 rows"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The cuts at k 4 have sizes 52, 44, 54, 30 for Ward, 51, 46, 54, 29 for complete and 96, 54, 29, 1 for average. Only average linkage exceeds 54, the largest core facies (limestone), and it leaves one row alone. A Ward cluster of 54 rows equals the limestone count and does not exceed it.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-agglomerative-clustering', 5, 'At k 4 the Ward cut''s next merge sits at 6.612020 and the complete-linkage cut''s at 2.970753. What can be said from those two heights?', '["Ward''s clusters sit about twice as far apart as the complete-linkage clusters do", "Nothing across the two: Ward heights are on another scale and are never compared with complete", "The complete-linkage cut is the better one, because its heights are smaller throughout", "That both trees split the rows alike, since both heights fall between 2 and 7"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A Ward height is the square root of twice the rise in the within-cluster sum of squares, and a complete height is a largest distance. The course states Ward''s heights are on a different scale from the other two and are never compared with them. A height is read against the other heights of its own tree.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-agglomerative-clustering', 6, 'Merge 175 of the Ward tree is the row [351, 354, 3.659186, 44]. Which id does the cluster it makes receive?', '["354, the larger of the two ids it merges", "44, the number of data rows the new cluster holds", "175, the number of the merge, which becomes the cluster''s own id", "355, since the cluster made at merge s gets id 180 + s"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis reads "scipy linkage matrix: rows 0 to n - 1, the cluster made at step s is n + s; each row [smaller id, larger id, height, size]", so merge 175 makes id 355. 354 is the cluster made at merge 174, which this merge absorbs, and 44 is the size column.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-agglomerative-clustering', 7, 'Which rows does the first merge of the Ward tree join, and why those?', '["Rows 24 and 63, both limestone, at 0.069063: the closest pair", "Rows 0 and 1, because merges run in file order", "Ids 348 and 357 at 30.809387, because the tree records its largest merge first", "Rows 13 and 102 at 0.111522, the pair that sorts first"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Merge 0 joins rows 24 and 63 at height 0.069063, the closest pair of all, making id 180. Rows 13 and 102 are merge 1 at 0.111522; ids 348 and 357 at 30.809387 are the last merge, 178, which joins all 180 rows.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-agglomerative-clustering', 8, 'A cut of the 180 cored rows at k 4 keeps how many merges of the tree?', '["4, one merge for each cluster the cut returns", "179, all of them, before relabelling the rows into 4 groups", "176, the first n - k", "175, the merges numbered up to the one the cut height sits above"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis reads "k clusters after the first n - k merges; clusters numbered 0 to k - 1 in the order of their first row", so 180 - 4 = 176 merges are kept: merges 0 to 175. The last kept is merge 175 at 3.659186 and the first undone is merge 176 at 6.612020.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-agglomerative-clustering', 9, 'How are the clusters of a cut numbered?', '["Largest first: the biggest cluster is 0 and the smallest k - 1", "By the height at which each cluster was last merged, lowest first", "Following their ids in the linkage matrix, the lowest id as 0", "0 to k - 1 in the order of their first row"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The cut''s basis numbers clusters 0 to k - 1 in the order of their first row, so cluster 0 is whichever holds row 0. The Ward cut at k 4 reads 52, 44, 54, 30 in cluster order, which is not sorted by size.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-agglomerative-clustering', 10, 'The Ward cut at k 4 keeps a last merge at 3.659186 and undoes a next merge at 6.612020. Which cutting heights give the same 4 clusters?', '["Only 3.659186 itself, the height of the last merge kept", "Any height between 3.659186 and 6.612020", "Any height above 6.612020, up to the root at 30.809387", "Any height between 2.814872 and 3.659186, below the merge that made the cluster of 44"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course reads the Ward cut: the last kept has height 3.659186 and the first undone 6.612020, so any cutting height between the two gives the same 4 clusters. Above 6.612020 merge 176 is kept and the count falls to 3; between 2.814872 and 3.659186 merge 175 is undone and the count is 5.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-agglomerative-clustering', 11, 'Going from the Ward cut at k 3 (96, 54, 30) to the cut at k 4 (52, 44, 54, 30), what happens?', '["The cluster of 96 splits into 52 and 44, and the other two are untouched", "The cluster of 54 splits in two and the 96 is shared out", "Every cluster is refitted from new centres, so all four differ from the three", "The cluster of 30 takes 22 rows from the 96, becoming the 44"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each step down the tree undoes one merge: at k 4 merge 176, which joined the 52 and the 44 into 96, is undone. A cluster of the k cut is always the union of clusters of the k + 1 cut, which the course checked on every pair of cuts from k 2 to 6.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-agglomerative-clustering', 12, 'At seed 3, how does the k-means clustering at k 4 relate to the k-means clustering at k 3?', '["Each cluster at k 4 lies wholly inside one cluster at k 3, as the tree''s do", "The k 4 run starts from the k 3 centres and adds one, so the clusters nest", "One shared mulberry32 stream makes the k 3 labels fix the k 4 ones", "One of the 4 clusters at k 4 has rows in more than one cluster at k 3"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course derives from the two label lists that one of the 4 k-means clusters at k 4 has rows in more than one cluster at k 3: k-means has no nesting rule. Each k is its own run from its own mulberry32(seed) stream and its own k-means++ starts.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-agglomerative-clustering', 13, 'cutTree is given the returned Ward matrix of the cored rows and k 5. What does it return?', '["Labels refitted from fresh centres, close to agglomerative''s at k 5 without matching them", "Clusters of 52, 22, 8, 14, 54 and 30 rows, the cut one merge further down", "The labels agglomerative gives at k 5, clusters of 52, 22, 22, 54 and 30 rows", "A refusal, since the matrix came from a call made with k 4"]'::jsonb, 2, 'cutTree gives exactly the labels agglomerative gives with that k, checked at k 2 to 6, and at k 5 the Ward sizes are 52, 22, 22, 54, 30. 52, 22, 8, 14, 54, 30 is the k 6 cut. The matrix holds the whole history whatever k the first call was given.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-agglomerative-clustering', 14, 'An agglomerative call asks for single linkage. What comes back?', '["A single-linkage tree, since the nearest pair of rows defines closeness", "The refusal \"linkage must be ''ward'', ''complete'' or ''average''\"", "Average linkage in its place, with a warning", "The Ward tree, since Ward is the default and an unknown name falls back"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine builds Ward, complete and average linkage and refuses any other by name, in its own words quoted in the key. It substitutes nothing and warns of nothing.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-agglomerative-clustering', 15, 'A hand-built matrix for four rows merges row 0 in both of its first two rows. What does cutTree do?', '["Refuses, naming linkageMatrix[1]: each id may be merged once only", "Accepts it, since cutTree reads only the heights and the sizes", "Refuses, naming linkageMatrix[0], the first row to use id 0", "Accepts it and ignores the second merge of id 0 when it cuts"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s words are "linkageMatrix[1] merges id 0, which linkageMatrix[0] already merged: each row id (0 to 3) and each cluster id (4 to 5) may be merged once only". It names the second row, where the reuse happens, and it neither ignores the merge nor reads the heights alone.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-matching-clusters-to-core', 1, 'In the contingency table of the teaching clustering against core, which cluster holds two facies?', '["Cluster 3, with 38 shaly-sand rows and 9 sandstone rows", "Cluster 0, holding all 29 shale rows and 9 shaly-sand rows beside them", "Cluster 1: all 50 sandstone rows and 9 shaly-sand rows", "None: each cluster holds one facies"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The table reads cluster 0: 29 shale; cluster 1: 50 sandstone and 9 shaly-sand; cluster 2: 54 limestone; cluster 3: 38 shaly-sand. Only cluster 1 mixes two facies, and shaly-sand is the facies split across two clusters.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-matching-clusters-to-core', 2, 'One-to-one matching maps the teaching clusters to shale, sandstone, limestone and shaly-sand. How many cored rows land on their own facies, and what accuracy is that?', '["171 of the 180, an accuracy of 0.950000", "All 180, since every cluster takes a facies of its own, so accuracy 1.000000", "The 171 in clusters 0, 2 and 3 alone, as cluster 1 is left out of the score", "171 once the 9 shaly-sand rows are dropped, so 0.983333 over the rows kept"]'::jsonb, 0, '171 of the 180 cored rows land on their own facies, accuracy 0.950000 (171 / 180). The 9 shaly-sand rows of cluster 1, named sandstone, are scored wrong and never dropped. The 50 sandstone rows of cluster 1 are among the 171, and 0.983333 is the majority accuracy at k 5.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-matching-clusters-to-core', 3, 'How does one-to-one matching choose which facies each cluster takes?', '["Greedily, each cluster in turn taking its best facies still free, in cluster order", "Each cluster takes its most common facies, and two clusters may share one name", "The facies whose mean logs sit nearest the cluster''s centre in log units takes it", "The assignment, a different facies per cluster, that matches the most rows (Hungarian)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis reads "one-to-one: each cluster to a different facies, maximising the rows matched (Hungarian); among equal totals the first mapping in cluster order that takes the first facies". Taking clusters one at a time is greedy matching, and letting clusters share a facies is majority matching.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-matching-clusters-to-core', 4, 'Facies a, b, a, b are matched one-to-one against clusters 0, 0, 1, 1. Both mappings match 2 rows. Which does the engine return?', '["Cluster 0 to b and cluster 1 to a, the mapping that sorts last", "Cluster 0 to a and cluster 1 to b", "A refusal naming clusters, since two mappings match the same count", "Both clusters to a, since a sorts first and one-to-one may repeat it"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Among equal totals the engine takes the first mapping in cluster order that takes the first facies, golden `match-tie-first-mapping`: cluster 0 to a, cluster 1 to b. A tie in the total is resolved by that rule and never refused, and one-to-one never gives two clusters one facies.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-matching-clusters-to-core', 5, 'The k 5 k-means clustering is passed to matchClusters in one-to-one mode. What comes back?', '["A refusal naming clusters: 5 clusters, more than the 4 core facies", "A mapping in which the smallest cluster, of 22 rows, is scored as wrong throughout", "The majority mapping, switched in by the engine", "Five matched clusters, shaly-sand taking two, with an accuracy of 0.983333"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s words are "clusters has 5 clusters, more than the 4 core facies: one-to-one matching would leave clusters unmatched; use mode ''majority'' or fewer clusters". It never switches mode for the caller; 0.983333 is the accuracy when the caller asks for majority.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-matching-clusters-to-core', 6, 'Under majority matching of the k 5 clustering, which facies takes two clusters, and what is the accuracy?', '["Sandstone, clusters 2 and 4; accuracy 0.950000", "None of them, since majority matching keeps one facies to a cluster", "Shaly-sand, clusters 3 and 4; accuracy 0.983333", "Limestone, clusters 1 and 2; accuracy 0.984510"]'::jsonb, 2, 'At k 5 the majority facies are shale, limestone, sandstone, shaly-sand and shaly-sand for clusters 0 to 4, so shaly-sand takes clusters 3 and 4, and the accuracy is 0.983333 with a macro F1 of 0.984510. 0.950000 is the one-to-one accuracy at k 4.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-matching-clusters-to-core', 7, 'Majority matching at k 5 scores 0.983333 against one-to-one at k 4, 0.950000. What does the higher figure show about k 5?', '["That k 5 is the better choice, since more cored rows land on their own facies there than at k 4", "Nothing in its favour: splitting a facies into two clusters costs majority matching nothing", "Shaly-sand is truly two facies, which the core description would have to confirm", "One-to-one matching is biased at k 4, and majority should replace it everywhere"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course states that splitting a facies into two clusters costs majority matching nothing, so a higher majority score at a larger k is no evidence for that k. Scores compare only at the same k and in the same mode.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-matching-clusters-to-core', 8, 'The k 3 k-means clustering is matched one-to-one. Which facies gets no cluster, and what happens to it?', '["Shale, the smallest facies at 29 rows, with a recall of 0.000000 and accuracy 0.738889", "No facies: fewer clusters than facies are refused", "Sandstone, which the three clusters split between them, scoring 0.808511", "Shaly-sand: recall 0.000000, and accuracy falls to 0.738889"]'::jsonb, 3, 'At k 3 one-to-one matching leaves shaly-sand with no cluster: its recall is 0.000000 and the accuracy is 0.738889. Fewer clusters than facies are accepted; only more clusters than facies are refused. 0.808511 is shaly-sand''s recall at k 4.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-matching-clusters-to-core', 9, 'Under the one-to-one mapping at k 4, why is sandstone''s precision below 1 while its recall is 1.000000?', '["Some core sandstone rows fall in cluster 3, which is named shaly-sand, so those rows are missed", "Precision divides by all 180 cored rows, while sandstone holds only 50 of those cored rows", "The 9 shaly-sand rows of cluster 1 are predicted sandstone, and every core sandstone row is in cluster 1", "The rows of the limestone cluster that read like sandstone lower its precision to 0.847458"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Sandstone''s precision is 0.847458: of the 59 rows predicted sandstone, 9 are core shaly-sand. Its recall is 1.000000 because all 50 core sandstone rows sit in cluster 1. Cluster 3 holds no sandstone and cluster 2 no sandstone; precision divides by the rows predicted, which are 59.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-matching-clusters-to-core', 10, 'Under the same mapping, what are shaly-sand''s precision and recall?', '["Precision 1.000000 and recall 0.808511", "Precision 0.808511 and recall 1.000000, the other way round", "Both 0.894118, since F1 sits between precision and recall and equals both here", "Precision 0.847458 and recall 0.808511, both lowered by the same 9 rows"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Cluster 3, the only cluster named shaly-sand, holds nothing else, so precision is 1.000000; 9 of its 47 core rows fall in cluster 1, so recall is 0.808511. 0.894118 is its F1 and 0.847458 is sandstone''s precision.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-matching-clusters-to-core', 11, 'What is the macro F1 of the one-to-one mapping of the teaching clustering?', '["0.950000, the accuracy", "0.952887", "0.894118, shaly-sand''s F1", "0.917431, the F1 of sandstone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The classification report gives F1 1.000000, 0.917431, 1.000000 and 0.894118 for the four facies, macro F1 0.952887 and accuracy 0.950000. The macro figure is a separate field from the accuracy and from any one facies''s F1.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-matching-clusters-to-core', 12, 'A matchClusters call passes mode ''best''. What does the engine say?', '["Nothing; it runs one-to-one, the mode it takes by default", "clusters must be an array of 180 labels, one per row", "It runs majority with a warning", "mode must be ''one-to-one'' or ''majority''"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine offers exactly two modes and refuses any other by name, in its own words quoted in the key. The one-per-row message is the refusal of a clusters list of the wrong length, and no mode is substituted.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-matching-clusters-to-core', 13, 'A matchClusters call sets zeroDivision to one half. What happens?', '["Accepted, and the half is then used wherever a ratio in the report would divide zero by zero", "Rounded to 1 with a warning", "A refusal by name: \"zeroDivision must be 0 or 1\"", "Accepted in majority mode only, and refused under one-to-one"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The machine learning engine''s scorer takes 0 or 1 for a ratio that divides zero by zero, and the engine refuses a half in the words quoted. At k 3 shaly-sand is never predicted, so its precision is exactly the case the setting decides.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-matching-clusters-to-core', 14, 'Under majority matching, a cluster holds equal counts of sandstone and shale. Which facies does it take?', '["Sandstone, the facies that sorts first", "Shale, since shale sorts ahead of sandstone by character", "Neither: the call is refused", "Shale, the rarer facies at 29 rows"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The majority basis reads "a tie goes to the facies that sorts first". Names sort by character, so the order is limestone, sandstone, shale, shaly-sand, and sandstone comes first. A tie is settled by that rule and never refused, and rarity plays no part.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-matching-clusters-to-core', 15, 'Cluster 1 of the k 5 run is its limestone cluster, while in the teaching clustering at k 4 limestone is cluster 2. Why?', '["The k 5 run numbers its clusters by facies name, and limestone is the facies that sorts first", "They are different runs, and a cluster number is only a name set by where the starting rows fell", "Adding a fifth centre renumbers the clusters, shifting each number along by one place", "Cluster numbers are sorted by size, and limestone is the second largest cluster of the k 5 run"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course reads cluster numbers as names: numbers are assigned by where the starting rows fell, and cluster 0 carries no meaning of its own. At k 5 cluster 0 is still the shale-only cluster, so nothing shifted by one; and the k 5 sizes, 29, 54, 51, 24, 22, are not in size order.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-adjusted-rand-index', 1, 'When does a pair of rows AGREE between two labellings, in the adjusted Rand index?', '["When the two rows carry the same name in both labellings", "When both labellings put the two rows together, or both put them apart", "Only when both labellings put the two rows together in one group", "When the two rows sit nearer each other than the mean distance"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The index counts pairs: a pair agrees when both labellings put it together or both put it apart, whatever the groups are called. Comparing names needs a mapping, and the index uses none; pairs put apart in both count as agreement too.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-adjusted-rand-index', 2, 'For a = 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1 and b = p, p, q, q, r, r, how many pairs does each labelling put together, and how many do both?', '["3 in a and 6 in b, 2 in both, of 15 pairs in all", "6 in a and 3 in b, so 9 together in both", "2 in a, 2 in b, 2 in both, of the 6 rows", "6 in a, 3 in b, 2 in both, of 15 pairs in all"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each group of a holds 3 rows, C(3, 2) = 3 pairs each, 6 in all; each group of b holds 2 rows, one pair each, 3 in all; the cells of 2 in the contingency table give sum C(n_ij, 2) = 2; and C(6, 2) = 15. Pairs together in both are counted from the cells, never by adding the two labellings'' counts.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-adjusted-rand-index', 3, 'For the same six rows, what is the expected count of pairs both labellings join by chance?', '["1.200000, from 6 x 3 / 15", "2, the pairs together in both that the table counts", "0.242424, the index once the expectation is taken off", "15, since every pair has a chance of falling together"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The basis gives E = sum C(a_i, 2) sum C(b_j, 2) / C(n, 2) = 6 x 3 / 15 = 1.200000. 2 is the actual count of pairs together in both, 0.242424 is the index itself, and 15 is the count of all pairs.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-adjusted-rand-index', 4, 'What adjusted Rand index does the engine return for those six rows?', '["2, the raw count of pairs both labellings join", "0.800000, the share of the 15 pairs that agree", "0.242424", "Minus the expectation, 1.200000"]'::jsonb, 2, '(2 - 1.200000) / ((6 + 3) / 2 - 1.200000) = 0.242424, and the engine returns 0.242424: the two labellings agree a little more than chance. The raw count and the expectation are pieces of the formula.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-adjusted-rand-index', 5, 'The stated golden a = 0, 0, 1, 1 against b = 0, 1, 0, 1 is scored. What comes back?', '["0.000000, since the engine clips any negative index up to 0", "1.000000, as the two labellings use the same two names", "-0.500000: every pair a joins, b splits, and the reverse", "A refusal naming b, since no pair is together in both"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Labellings that disagree more than chance go below 0, and golden `ari-negative` scores -0.500000. The engine reports a negative index as it is; the names are irrelevant to a pair count; and no pair together in both is a legitimate count of 0.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-adjusted-rand-index', 6, 'Two labellings each put every row in one cluster. What does the engine return, and why is a rule needed?', '["1.000000; the formula divides zero by zero, and the engine follows scikit-learn", "0.000000, since two single clusters agree no better than chance would", "A refusal naming a, because one cluster has no pair to disagree on", "An undefined value, printed as none, since the formula cannot be taken"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The basis reads "when the denominator is zero (both labelings one cluster, or both all singletons) the index is 1 (scikit-learn)", golden `ari-both-one-cluster`. The engine neither refuses the call nor returns an empty value.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-adjusted-rand-index', 7, 'The teaching partition comes back numbered differently at seed 1. What does the index read between seed 1 and seed 3, and against core?', '["1.000000 between them, and 0.872413 each against the core", "0.000000 between them, since no row keeps its number", "0.863179 between them, and 0.873388 for each against the core", "1.000000 between them, with seed 1 at 0.897678 against the core"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Seed 1 is the teaching partition with other numbers, so the index between them is 1.000000 and both score 0.872413 against the core. 0.863179 is k-means against Ward; 0.873388 is Ward against core and 0.897678 complete against core.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-adjusted-rand-index', 8, 'One-to-one matching of the teaching clustering returns an adjusted Rand index beside its mapped scores. How does it relate to the mapping?', '["0.950000, the accuracy of the mapped facies, stated a second way", "It rises as the mapping improves", "0.952887, the macro F1 of the four mapped facies read as pairs", "It is 0.872413, the same figure, and uses no mapping at all"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis reads "adjusted Rand index of the clusters against the facies (independent of the mapping)", and it returns 0.872413, the figure adjustedRandIndex gives. 0.950000 is the mapped accuracy and 0.952887 the macro F1, both of which depend on the mapping.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-adjusted-rand-index', 9, 'The teaching k-means and the Ward cut at k 4 are compared with each other, neither being the core. What does the index read?', '["1.000000, since both agree with the core almost equally", "0.863179: close, and below 1, so they are two different groupings", "0.873388, the Ward figure, since a comparison takes the higher of the two", "Nothing, since the index needs one labelling to be the core facies"]'::jsonb, 1, 'k-means against Ward scores 0.863179. The index needs no core; either labelling can be any grouping. Two methods can each score close to the core (0.872413 and 0.873388) and still differ from each other, and only an index of 1.000000 marks the same grouping.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-adjusted-rand-index', 10, 'Of the four k 4 methods on the cored rows, which agrees best with the core by the adjusted Rand index?', '["Ward, at 0.873388, the tree that minimises sums of squares", "Complete linkage, 0.897678", "k-means at seed 3, 0.872413, the method with the highest silhouette", "Average linkage, 0.676404, whose cut holds the largest cluster"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The table reads k-means 0.872413, Ward 0.873388, complete 0.897678 and average 0.676404; complete agrees best here. Average linkage is lowest of the four, and a high silhouette is no ranking against core.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-adjusted-rand-index', 11, 'Grouping each cored row by the well it came from scores 0.157021 against the core facies. What does the course read into that figure?', '["It is a grouping that knows nothing about the rock", "Wells differ in rock, so the well is a fair first guess at facies", "Six groups against four break the index", "It shows the four methods at k 4 each found about a sixth of the rock"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course states that the well names group the rows too and score 0.157021, a grouping that knows nothing about the rock. The index accepts groupings of any size on each side, and the four methods score from 0.676404 to 0.897678.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-adjusted-rand-index', 12, 'Ward scores 0.873388 against the core and k-means 0.872413. Which statement is supported by the figures?', '["Both print the same index to three decimals, so they are one grouping of the cored rows", "k-means prints the higher index, since its mean silhouette of 0.545063 is the higher one", "Ward prints the higher index, and against each other the two score 0.863179, so they differ", "A tie, since the two indices print so close together that neither can lead"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ward reads 0.873388 and k-means 0.872413, so Ward prints the higher figure. Against each other they score 0.863179, below 1, so they are different groupings; printing alike would not make them one. The silhouette is a separate measure.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-adjusted-rand-index', 13, 'An adjustedRandIndex call passes two labellings of one row each. What does the engine say?', '["b must be an array of 180 labels, one per row", "a must be an array of at least 2 labels", "It returns 1.000000, as a single row agrees with itself", "It returns 0.000000, as one row holds no pair"]'::jsonb, 1, 'One row holds no pair to count, so the index cannot be taken: the engine names the field a and states its floor of 2 labels. The 180-label message is a separate check, raised for a list of the wrong length, and no value is returned for a single row.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-adjusted-rand-index', 14, 'On the 180 cored rows, the second labelling handed to the index is one label short. Which refusal comes back?', '["It scores the 179 rows the two lists share", "a must be an array of at least 2 labels, since the lists differ", "It pads b with its last label and warns", "b must be an array of 180 labels, one per row"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every row needs a label on both sides, so 179 labels against 180 rows is refused by name, in the engine''s words quoted in the key. Nothing is trimmed or padded, and the other message offered concerns a list too short to hold a single pair.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-adjusted-rand-index', 15, 'At k 4, the mean silhouette ranks k-means first and complete third, while the index against core ranks complete first. What explains the difference?', '["The silhouette scores compact, separated groups; the index scores agreement with the rock", "The silhouette is computed on raw logs and the index on standardised logs", "The index uses a mapping of clusters to facies, and the silhouette does not", "Complete linkage has more clusters than k-means at k 4, which the index rewards"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The silhouettes at k 4 read k-means 0.545063, Ward 0.531627, complete 0.530601 and average 0.514177; the indices against core read complete 0.897678 first. The silhouette measures compactness and separation and says nothing of rock types. Both run on the standardised logs, the index uses no mapping, and every method here has 4 clusters.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-electrofacies-against-core', 1, 'In the five-step judging workflow on the cored wells, which step is the first to take the core facies as an input?', '["Step 1, since the elbow scores each k against the four facies", "Step 3, where each labelling''s silhouette is set against the core", "Step 4, matchClusters one-to-one", "Step 2, where k-means is seeded from one row of each facies"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s function table lists the elbow''s inputs as X, kMin, kMax, seed, nInit, scale and withSilhouette, kmeans and agglomerative take no facies, and silhouette takes the labels under test; matchClusters is the first call given yTrue. Clusters are made from logs alone before the core enters.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-electrofacies-against-core', 2, 'What are the mean silhouettes of the Ward and complete-linkage cuts at k 4, beside k-means at 0.545063?', '["Ward 0.531627, complete 0.530601", "Ward 0.873388, complete 0.897678, the figures of each cut", "Both 0.514177, the figure shared by every agglomerative cut", "Ward 0.599453, complete 0.438615, read from the cluster table"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The workflow prints the Ward and complete silhouettes at k 4 as 0.531627 and 0.530601. 0.873388 and 0.897678 are their adjusted Rand indices against core; 0.514177 is the average-linkage cut alone; and 0.599453 and 0.438615 are k-means clusters 0 and 1.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-electrofacies-against-core', 3, 'Step 1 of the workflow reads the elbow with its silhouette. What does it return on the cored rows?', '["An elbow picked at k 4 by the drop fractions, with the best silhouette also at k 4", "Drop fractions 0.659179, 0.668972, 0.282430 at k 3, 4, 5, and the best silhouette at k 2 instead", "The drops 164.159721, 22.942083, 6.546018 at k 3, 4, 5, and no silhouette until step 3", "Drop fractions 0.668972, 0.282430, 0.112303 at k 3, 4, 5, and the best silhouette at k 3"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The workflow table prints drop fractions 0.668972, 0.282430 and 0.112303 at k 3, 4 and 5, and the best silhouette at k 3. No elbow is picked automatically; 0.659179 is the k 2 fraction; and the silhouette at each k comes from the elbow call when withSilhouette is set.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-electrofacies-against-core', 4, 'Across its four logs, where does shaly-sand''s mean sit against the other core facies?', '["Beside limestone''s on RHOB and PEF, which is why it overlaps limestone in the clusters", "Between sandstone''s mean and shale''s mean on every one of the four logs", "Above shale''s on GR, at 117.406897, so gamma ray alone sets it apart", "On sandstone''s on every log, since the two facies share one mean"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The mean-log table reads shaly-sand GR 65.208511, RHOB 2.397043, NPHI 0.241766 and PEF 2.222553, each between sandstone''s (46.314000, 2.317520, 0.198340, 1.956800) and shale''s (117.406897, 2.510793, 0.337069, 3.213793). 117.406897 is shale''s own GR mean.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-electrofacies-against-core', 5, 'Which three figures from the course point at shaly-sand as the facies the logs barely separate?', '["Its silhouette 0.245762, its recall 0.000000 at k 4, and 38 of 47 rows in the sandstone cluster of the teaching run", "Its F1 of 0.917431, its precision 0.847458, and the 9 sandstone rows that fell into cluster 3", "Its core-facies silhouette 0.152252, its recall 0.808511, and 9 of 47 rows in the sandstone cluster", "An index of 0.676404 against core, its 96-row cluster, and 29 of its rows alone in cluster 0"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Shaly-sand has the lowest core-facies silhouette, 0.152252, and a recall of 0.808511 under the k-means mapping, with 9 of its 47 rows in the sandstone cluster. 0.245762 is cluster 3''s silhouette; 0.917431 and 0.847458 are sandstone''s F1 and precision; 0.676404 is average linkage''s index.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-electrofacies-against-core', 6, 'The write-up states how k was chosen. What does it say?', '["The core describes 4 facies, with the elbow''s drop fractions and the silhouette at k 2 to 8 printed beside the choice", "k 3 was chosen because both the drop fraction and the silhouette peak there, and the core count was set aside", "k 4 was picked by the engine''s elbow, which the silhouette then confirmed with its best mean figure at k 4", "Nothing about k, since a stated k needs no evidence once the clusters are matched to the core facies"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The write-up table reads "the core describes 4 facies; the elbow''s drop fractions and the silhouette at k 2 to 8 are printed beside the choice". The engine picks no elbow, and the best silhouette is at k 3, so the evidence that pointed elsewhere is printed with the choice.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-electrofacies-against-core', 7, 'Which line belongs in the write-up''s "what is not claimed"?', '["No agreement with core, since the clusters never saw it", "No accuracy for k-means, since matching is a choice", "No methods other than k-means, since the trees need no seed", "No facies for an uncored well"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The write-up table''s last line opens "no facies for an uncored well". The same write-up does state agreement with core (the indices) and the one-to-one accuracy 0.950000, and it compares Ward, complete and average linkage beside k-means.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-electrofacies-against-core', 8, 'The write-up''s "where it fails" line is stated in rows. What does it say?', '["average linkage: 96 rows in one cluster and one row alone at k 4", "shaly-sand: 9 of 47 rows fall with sandstone", "sandstone: 9 of its 50 rows fall with shaly-sand", "limestone: its 54 rows split into two clusters at k 5"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The table reads "shaly-sand: 9 of 47 rows fall with sandstone". Sandstone''s 50 rows all sit in cluster 1 (recall 1.000000), and limestone keeps one cluster of 54 at k 5 under majority matching. Average linkage''s sizes are a finding about one method, recorded with its index of 0.676404.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-electrofacies-against-core', 9, 'Of the four k 4 labellings, which ranks first by mean silhouette, and which by the index against core?', '["Complete linkage first on both measures, at 0.530601 and at 0.897678 against core", "k-means first by both, at 0.545063 and 0.872413", "k-means first by silhouette, 0.545063; complete first by index, 0.897678", "Ward first by silhouette, 0.531627; average linkage first by index, 0.676404"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The silhouettes read k-means 0.545063, Ward 0.531627, complete 0.530601, average 0.514177; the indices read complete 0.897678, Ward 0.873388, k-means 0.872413, average 0.676404. The two measures rank the methods differently because one scores compactness and the other agreement with rock.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-electrofacies-against-core', 10, 'The write-up gives both the index against core and the one-to-one accuracy for k-means. Which of the two depends on a choice of mapping?', '["The accuracy, 0.950000; the index, 0.872413, uses no mapping", "The index, 0.872413, since it pairs each cluster with one facies", "Neither, since both are computed on the same 180 cored rows alone", "Both, since each needs the clusters named as facies first"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The one-to-one accuracy scores mapped predictions, so its mode is named; the index''s basis reads "adjusted Rand index of the clusters against the facies (independent of the mapping)". Counting the same rows does not make two figures depend on the same choices.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-electrofacies-against-core', 11, 'Why does the agglomerative half of step 2 carry no seed in the write-up while k-means carries seed 3 and 10 starts?', '["Its seed is shared with the k-means call, so stating seed 3 once covers both methods", "The agglomerative call has no seed input, and each tree is named by its linkage", "Its seed is chosen by the linkage, Ward drawing one and the others none", "A seed would be quoted, but the trees used the default of 3 as well"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The function table lists agglomerative''s inputs as X, linkage, k, scale and names, and the write-up names each tree only by its linkage. k-means carries its seed and starts because another seed could stop elsewhere, as seed 5 does at 58.297079 with ten starts.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-electrofacies-against-core', 12, 'At k 5 under majority matching, shaly-sand takes two clusters of its own and accuracy rises to 0.983333. What does that show?', '["That k 5 is the right k, since the accuracy beats the k 4 figure of 0.950000", "The core description is wrong, and shaly-sand is two separate facies", "The sandstone cluster was the cause, since sandstone takes two clusters at k 5", "Two nearly pure shaly-sand clusters, 24 of 24 and 21 of 22 rows, and no case for k 5"]'::jsonb, 3, 'At k 5 majority matching gives shaly-sand clusters 3 and 4 (24 of 24 and 21 of 22 rows) and 0.983333. Splitting a facies costs majority matching nothing, so the higher score is no evidence for k 5, and sandstone takes one cluster, cluster 2.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-electrofacies-against-core', 13, 'On which logs is shale''s mean the highest of the four core facies?', '["RHOB and PEF, at 2.510793 and 3.213793", "GR alone, at 117.406897; limestone leads on the other three logs", "GR and NPHI, at 117.406897 gAPI and 0.337069 v/v", "GR and PEF, at 117.406897 gAPI and 3.213793 b/e respectively"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The facies means read GR 26.879630, 46.314000, 117.406897, 65.208511 and NPHI 0.069037, 0.198340, 0.337069, 0.241766 for limestone, sandstone, shale and shaly-sand, so shale is highest on both. Limestone leads on RHOB, 2.642481, and PEF, 4.909259, and leads on neither GR nor NPHI.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-electrofacies-against-core', 14, 'Why should the average-linkage mean silhouette at k 4 always travel with its cluster sizes?', '["0.514177 on its own hides a cluster of 96 rows and a row alone", "Because 0.514177 is refused by the engine unless the sizes are attached", "Its sizes decide the scaling the silhouette uses", "Sizes turn 0.514177 into an index against core"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The average-linkage cut scores 0.514177 with clusters of 96, 54, 29 and 1; the mean alone gives no hint of the large cluster or the lone row, which the sizes show at once. The silhouette is scored on the standardised logs whatever the sizes, and no size makes it an agreement with rock.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-electrofacies-against-core', 15, 'Which rows and logs does the write-up name for the comparison with core?', '["All 240 rows of the eight wells, with CALI kept as a fifth log for the hole size", "The 180 cored rows of EKENE-1 to EKENE-6; GR, RHOB, NPHI and PEF, standard scaling", "The 180 cored rows on the raw logs, so that gamma ray keeps its own units", "The 30 rows of EKENE-1 alone, each log standardised on those rows"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The write-up table reads "180 cored rows of EKENE-1, EKENE-2, EKENE-3, EKENE-4, EKENE-5, EKENE-6; GR, RHOB, NPHI, PEF, standard scaling". Only cored rows can be compared with core, CALI carries no rock signal, and every figure in the comparison is on the standardised logs.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 1, 'On the seed 3 elbow of the cored rows, what are the drop and the drop fraction at k 5?', '["A drop of 6.546018, a fraction 0.079256 of the k 5 inertia itself", "4.100956 as the drop, with 0.112303 as the fraction", "A drop of 6.546018, a fraction 0.112303", "22.942083 fallen, a fraction 0.282430 of what remained"]'::jsonb, 2, 'At k 5 the inertia falls from 58.289042 to 51.743024, a drop of 6.546018, and the fraction divides by the k 4 inertia to give 0.112303. 0.079256 is the k 6 fraction and 4.100956 the k 6 drop; 22.942083 and 0.282430 belong to k 4.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 2, 'The seed 3 elbow prints a drop of 3.092553 at k 8 after 2.686056 at k 7. Is that an inertia rise?', '["No: the inertia still falls, to 41.863457; only the drop grew", "Yes, and the engine warns that the k 8 runs stopped in a local minimum", "Yes: a larger drop at a larger k marks a poor stop", "It is, and the k 8 fraction prints as none"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A rise means a larger k prints a HIGHER inertia; here k 8 reads 41.863457, below the 44.956011 of k 7, so nothing is flagged. The larger drop lifts the k 8 fraction to 0.068791 above the k 7 0.056380, which is why the column does not fall smoothly.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 3, 'Nine seeds of the ten-start table reach the best partition''s inertia. Which seed is the exception, and what does it print?', '["Seed 7, at 78.775612, like seed 5 with one start", "Seed 10, stopping at 75.911640", "Seed 3 once more, at 58.330411", "Seed 5, at 58.297079"]'::jsonb, 3, 'With ten starts 9 of the 10 seeds reach 58.289042 and seed 5 stops at 58.297079. 78.775612, 75.911640 and 58.330411 are one-start figures, for seeds 7, 10 and 3.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 4, 'In the ten starts at k 4, seed 3, three starts print 58.289042. Which start keeps the win?', '["Start 8, the last of the three, since a later equal run replaces the best so far", "Start 3, the earliest; their largest difference from the winner is printed as 0", "Start 5, which needed the fewest passes of the three to reach the figure", "None of them, since three equal starts make the result ambiguous and it is refused"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course prints the largest difference between the three and the winner as 0, and a run that only matches the best so far does not replace it, so the earliest, start 3, keeps the win with 6 passes. Nothing is refused; start 5 took 5 passes and start 8 took 6.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 5, 'At seed 5, k-means with one start stops at 78.775612. What cluster sizes does it return?', '["The teaching clustering''s 29, 59, 54 and 38 rows", "32, 96, 30 and 22 rows", "54, 50, 29 and 47 rows, the facies counts", "67, 24, 65 and 24 rows, as on the raw logs"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course prints clusters of 32, 96, 30, 22 rows at seed 5 with one start. 29, 59, 54, 38 is the ten-start teaching clustering, 54, 50, 29, 47 the core facies counts and 67, 24, 65, 24 the raw-log clustering.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 6, 'How many distinct inertias do the ten starts at k 4, seed 3, print at six decimals?', '["10, one per start", "3, the starts that tie", "4, one for each cluster", "6"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The 10 starts print 6 distinct inertias: 58.330411, 75.958349, 58.289042, 78.982664, 78.768801 and 77.624096. Three starts print 58.289042 and three print 58.330411.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 7, 'With the core facies used as labels in the standardised space, which facies scores the highest mean silhouette?', '["Limestone, 0.842345", "Shale, at 0.621976", "Sandstone, which reads 0.489765", "Shaly-sand, at 0.152252"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The core-facies means read limestone 0.842345, sandstone 0.489765, shale 0.621976 and shaly-sand 0.152252, with 0.528711 over all 180 rows.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 8, 'A silhouette call asks for a sample larger than the 180 cored rows. What does the engine say?', '["Scores all 180 rows instead, cutting the sample down to the rows passed", "Draws the extra rows again from mulberry32, repeating some of them", "A refusal naming sampleSize, whose range here runs from 2 to 180", "labels must be an array of 180 labels, one per row"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine refuses by name in its own words, "sampleSize must be a whole number from 2 to 180": a sample runs from 2 rows to the rows passed. It neither clips the request nor draws repeats, and the labels message is a different check.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 9, 'A silhouette call asks for a 60-row sample and passes no seed. What happens?', '["Uses seed 3, the course''s teaching seed, and scores 0.521590", "It scores the first 60 rows in file order, with no shuffle at all", "Ignores the sample, scoring all 180 rows at 0.545063", "Refused, naming seed: a sample needs one from 0 to 4294967295"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A sampled silhouette draws its rows from a mulberry32(seed) shuffle, so a sample with no seed is refused by name, in the engine''s words "seed must be a whole number from 0 to 4294967295". No default seed is taken; 0.521590 is what seed 3 gives when it is passed.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 10, 'Row 56 of the cored rows sits alone in cluster 3 of the average-linkage cut at k 4. Where does it come from, and what does it score?', '["EKENE-1 at 6228 ft, scoring -0.045137", "EKENE-5 at 6462 ft, at -0.002304", "Shaly-sand of EKENE-2 at 6306 ft, scoring 0.000000", "Limestone of EKENE-6 at 6526 ft, scoring 0.842778 like its cluster"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Row 56 is shaly-sand of EKENE-2 at 6306 ft, and as a row alone in its cluster it scores 0. The other depths are rows 12, 135 and 169 of the k-means clustering, which score below 0; 0.842778 is k-means cluster 2''s mean.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 11, 'What range of values can one row''s silhouette take?', '["From -1 to 1", "From 0 up to 1 only", "Anywhere from -1 to 0", "From 0 to the largest distance between rows"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The silhouette is (b - a) / max(a, b), from -1 to 1. The teaching clustering has rows below 0, the lowest at -0.045137, and division by max(a, b) removes the units of distance.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 12, 'Which cut of the Ward tree splits the 180 cored rows into clusters of 126 and 54?', '["k 3, undoing merge 177 at 18.119587", "k 2, undoing the last merge, at 30.809387", "k 2, undoing merge 176 at 6.612020, the 96-row cluster", "k 4, where the cut undoes the 96 at 6.612020"]'::jsonb, 1, 'At k 2 the Ward cut reads 126, 54 and the merge undone first is the last, 178, at 30.809387. At k 3 it reads 96, 54, 30, undoing merge 177 at 18.119587; merge 176 at 6.612020 is undone first at k 4.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 13, 'Merge 177 of the Ward tree joins ids 353 and 356 at 18.119587. How many rows does the cluster it makes hold, and which cut undoes it first?', '["96 rows, undone first by the cut at k 4", "All 180 rows, which the cut at k 2 undoes first", "126 rows; the cut at k 3", "126 rows, undone first at k 2"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Merge 177 makes id 357 of 126 rows, and the k 3 cut, whose merge undone first sits at 18.119587, splits it. The k 2 cut undoes merge 178 first at 30.809387, and the 96-row cluster is merge 176.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 14, 'Complete linkage on the standardised logs: which sizes and heights describe its four-cluster cut?', '["Sizes 51, 46, 54, 29; last merge made 2.205317, next merge 2.970753", "Sizes 52, 44, 54, 30; last merge made 3.659186, next merge up 6.612020", "96, 54, 29, 1, with heights 1.273129 below and 1.346979 above the cut", "The same 51, 46, 54, 29, but 2.970753 below and 2.205317 above"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The complete-linkage cut at k 4 reads 51, 46, 54, 29 with cut heights 2.205317 (the last merge made) and 2.970753 (the next merge). The Ward and average rows carry the other figures, and the last merge made always sits below the next one on these trees, whose heights never fall.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 15, 'An agglomerative call on six rows asks for seven clusters. What does the engine say?', '["It returns six clusters, one per row, and a warning about k", "k must be a whole number from 1 to 5 (the number of merges)", "It merges nothing and returns every row as cluster 0", "It refuses and names k: on six rows a cut runs from 1 to 6 clusters"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A cut runs from k 1, with nothing undone, to one cluster per row, with every merge undone; seven clusters of six rows is refused in the engine''s own words, "k must be a whole number from 1 to 6 (the number of rows)".'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 16, 'A hand-built linkage matrix names, in its second row, a cluster id that no merge has yet made. What does cutTree do?', '["It builds the missing cluster from the rows its id would hold and cuts", "Refuses, naming linkageMatrix[1] and the whole ids that row may hold", "linkageMatrix must be the non-empty linkageMatrix of agglomerative", "A refusal naming linkageMatrix[0], since the ids begin there"]'::jsonb, 1, 'cutTree checks a matrix it is given and names the row at fault, counted from 0; on a small hand-built matrix its words are "linkageMatrix[1] must be [id1, id2, height, size] with whole ids 0 <= id1 < id2 < 4". The non-empty message is the refusal of an empty matrix, and nothing is built for the caller.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 17, 'cutTree is handed the Ward tree of the cored rows with k 0. What comes back?', '["One cluster of all 180 rows, the root", "180 clusters, one per row", "k must be a whole number from 1 to 179 (the merges in the tree)", "k must be a whole number from 1 to 180 (the number of rows)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine refuses k 0 by name in its own words: a cut runs from 1 to the number of rows. It substitutes neither the root nor the singletons, and the range counts rows, 180, where the matrix holds 179 merges.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 18, 'On the 180 cored rows, what do the merge heights of Ward, complete and average linkage do from one merge to the next?', '["They fall once, at the last merge, where the two largest clusters join", "Ward''s rise throughout; complete and average heights can fall between merges", "They never fall, so each tree can be drawn with every merge above its parts", "They hold level at 0.069063 through the first five merges of each tree"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course states that on these rows the merge heights of all three linkages never fall from one merge to the next. The first five Ward merges run from 0.069063 to 0.117328, and the last Ward merge, at 30.809387, is the highest.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 19, 'Rows 54 and 96 are joined early in the Ward history. At which step, at what height, and under what new id?', '["Merge 3, at 0.117222, making id 183", "Step 4, at 0.117328, which makes id 184", "Merge 3 at 0.117222, making id 3 after the merge number", "Merge 183, at 0.117222, made from ids 54 and 96"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Ward matrix row for merge 3 reads 54, 96, 0.117222, 2, and the cluster made at merge s is id 180 + s, so id 183. Merge 4 joins rows 20 and 127; merge numbers run 0 to 178, so there is no merge 183.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 20, 'In the Ward cut at k 4, which cluster number does row 0 of the cored rows carry?', '["Cluster 3, the cluster of 30 rows", "Cluster 2, whichever holds limestone", "Cluster 1, set by the height order", "Cluster 0"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A cut numbers its clusters 0 to k - 1 in the order of their first row, so the cluster holding row 0 is cluster 0, whatever its facies or height. The number is a name and says nothing of rock.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 21, 'Read down the shaly-sand column of the contingency table for the teaching clustering. Where did its 47 rows go?', '["All 47 in cluster 3", "38 to cluster 3 and 9 to cluster 1", "38 to cluster 1, with the other 9 in cluster 3", "24 in cluster 3 and 21 in 4"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The shaly-sand column reads 0, 9, 0, 38 for clusters 0 to 3, so 38 rows in cluster 3 and 9 in cluster 1. 24 and 21 are shaly-sand''s two clusters in the k 5 run, a different clustering.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 22, 'Under majority matching of the k 5 clustering, cluster 2 is named sandstone. How pure is it?', '["50 of 59, as in the k 4 run", "Every one of its 54 rows is sandstone", "49 of its 51 rows are sandstone", "21 of 22"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The k 5 majority table reads cluster 2: 49 sandstone rows of 51. 50 of 59 is cluster 1 of the teaching clustering; 54 of 54 is the k 5 limestone cluster; 21 of 22 is cluster 4, named shaly-sand.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 23, 'Under the one-to-one mapping at k 4, which two facies score 1.000000 on precision, recall and F1 alike?', '["Sandstone together with shaly-sand", "Limestone together with sandstone", "Shale and shaly-sand, the pair", "Limestone and shale"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Limestone (cluster 2) and shale (cluster 0) each sit alone in a cluster of their own, and score 1.000000 throughout. Sandstone''s precision is 0.847458 and shaly-sand''s recall 0.808511.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 24, 'Why does one-to-one matching of the k 3 clustering give shaly-sand a recall of 0.000000?', '["The engine drops shaly-sand from the scoring whenever k is below 4", "No cluster is named shaly-sand, so none of its rows can be predicted as it", "The zeroDivision setting of 0 forces the recall of shaly-sand to zero", "Its 47 rows are refused, since fewer clusters than facies are passed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Three clusters can carry only three facies names, and shaly-sand is the one without a cluster, so every one of its 47 rows is predicted as some other facies. Nothing is dropped or refused; the zeroDivision setting governs precision of a facies never predicted, and recall here has rows to divide by.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 25, 'matchClusters is given 179 cluster labels for the 180 cored rows. What does the engine say?', '["It scores the 179 rows that carry a cluster label and leaves the last row unscored", "a must be an array of at least 2 labels", "A refusal naming clusters, which must hold one label for each of the 180 rows", "It pads the list with cluster 0 so that every row has a label, and warns that it did"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine refuses a list one short in its own words, "clusters must be an array of 180 labels, one per row". It trims nothing and pads nothing, and the at-least-2 message belongs to adjustedRandIndex.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 26, 'Under one-to-one matching, can two clusters take the same facies?', '["No: each cluster takes a different facies", "Yes, when both clusters are mostly that facies", "Yes, once k reaches 5 or more clusters", "Only when two mappings tie on rows matched"]'::jsonb, 0, 'One-to-one gives each cluster a different facies, maximising rows matched; at k 5 it is refused. Sharing a facies is majority matching, and a tie between mappings is settled by the first mapping in cluster order.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 27, 'What does an adjusted Rand index near 0 say about two labellings?', '["They share no pair of rows at all", "One of the two labellings is a single cluster", "They disagree more than chance", "They are no more alike than chance"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The index is 1 for the same grouping, near 0 for labellings no more alike than chance, and below 0 when they disagree more than chance, as the golden with -0.500000 does. Two single clusters score 1.000000.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 28, 'Which contingency table does the engine print for a = 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1 against b = p, p, q, q, r, r?', '["Rows of 3, 0, 0 and 0, 3, 0", "Row 0: 2, 1, 0; row 1: 0, 1, 2", "Row 0 reads 2, 2, 0 and row 1 reads 0, 0, 2", "Every cell 1"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Rows 0 to 2 of a carry p, p, q, so row 0 reads p 2, q 1, r 0; rows 3 to 5 carry q, r, r, so row 1 reads 0, 1, 2. Each row sums to 3 and the columns to 2, 2, 2.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 29, 'When does the engine''s adjusted Rand index come out as 1 because the formula''s denominator is zero?', '["When both labellings are one cluster, or both are all singletons", "When both are one cluster, or one of the two is all singletons", "Whenever the labellings hold fewer than 2 rows between them", "When the two labellings match row for row, whatever their shape"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two one-cluster labellings, or two all-singleton labellings, give a zero denominator, and the engine returns 1 there as scikit-learn does. A call with fewer than 2 rows is refused by name, and identical labellings of any other shape reach 1 through the formula itself.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 30, 'Which pair of facts belongs to the average-linkage cut at k 4 on the cored rows?', '["Clusters of 52, 44, 54 and 30 rows, and an index of 0.873388 against core", "The same 96, 54, 29 and 1, but an index of 0.897678 against core", "Clusters of 96, 54, 29 and 1 rows, and an index of 0.676404 against core", "Clusters of 51, 46, 54 and 29, with an index of 0.676404 against core"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The average-linkage cut reads 96, 54, 29, 1 and scores 0.676404 against core. 52, 44, 54, 30 with 0.873388 is Ward, and 51, 46, 54, 29 is complete, which scores 0.897678.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 31, 'Cluster 3 of the teaching clustering has the lowest cluster silhouette, 0.245762. What does the contingency table say of it?', '["It holds the 9 sandstone rows that pull its silhouette down", "Its 38 rows are spread across all four facies", "It holds the 29 shale rows and 9 shaly-sand beside them", "All 38 of its rows are core shaly-sand"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The table reads cluster 3: 38 shaly-sand and nothing else, so the weakest cluster by silhouette is pure against core. The mixed cluster is cluster 1, 50 sandstone and 9 shaly-sand; the 29 shale rows sit alone in cluster 0.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 32, 'The largest drop fraction and bestSilhouetteK both point to k 3. What happens when the k 3 k-means clustering is matched one-to-one?', '["Every facies finds a cluster, and 0.950000 of the rows land on their facies", "It leaves one facies, shaly-sand, with no cluster, and 0.738889 of the rows match", "The engine refuses, since three clusters are fewer than the four facies", "Shale is left out, as the smallest facies, with 0.738889 matched"]'::jsonb, 1, 'At k 3 one-to-one matching leaves shaly-sand with no cluster and an accuracy of 0.738889 over the 180 cored rows. Fewer clusters than facies are accepted; 0.950000 is the k 4 accuracy. The k both measures prefer loses a whole facies.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 33, 'Which labelling at k 4 holds a row that scores 0.000000 because it sits alone in its cluster?', '["The average-linkage cut: its cluster 3 is row 56 alone", "The Ward cut, with a lone shale row", "The teaching k-means, whose cluster 3 of 38 rows scores 0 on average", "Complete linkage, isolating row 12"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The average-linkage cut has sizes 96, 54, 29, 1, and its lone row 56 scores 0 by the stated rule. Ward''s smallest cluster holds 30 rows and complete''s 29; k-means cluster 3 averages 0.245762; row 12 is a k-means row scoring -0.045137.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 34, 'Ward and complete linkage both cut the cored rows at k 4. Which agrees better with the core, and what can be said of their cut heights?', '["Ward on both counts: 0.873388, and the wider gap from 3.659186 to 6.612020", "Complete on both: 0.897678, and its lower heights make the sharper cut of the two", "Complete, 0.897678 against 0.873388; the two sets of heights are on different scales", "Ward agrees better, while complete leaves the wider gap between its two heights"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Against core complete reads 0.897678 and Ward 0.873388. Ward''s cut sits between 3.659186 and 6.612020 and complete''s between 2.205317 and 2.970753, yet those ranges belong to different measures of height, so neither gap can rank the trees.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 35, 'The seed 1 run returns the teaching partition with other cluster numbers. What changes in its contingency table and its index against core?', '["The index falls to 0.863179, since every cluster name moved", "Every count in the table moves, since no row keeps its number", "Its index against core reads 1.000000, as the runs agree", "Only the numbering of the table''s rows; the index stays 0.872413"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every cluster of one run is exactly one cluster of the other, so the counts are the same in another row order, and against the core both runs score 0.872413. 1.000000 is seed 1 against seed 3; 0.863179 is k-means against Ward.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 36, 'What is shaly-sand''s mean GR on the cored rows, and between which facies does it fall?', '["46.314000 gAPI, between limestone''s mean and shale''s mean", "65.208511 gAPI, between sandstone''s 46.314000 and shale''s 117.406897", "65.208511 gAPI, between limestone''s 26.879630 and sandstone''s 46.314000", "117.406897 gAPI, above every other facies on the log"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The facies means for GR read limestone 26.879630, sandstone 46.314000, shale 117.406897 and shaly-sand 65.208511. 46.314000 is sandstone''s own mean and 117.406897 shale''s.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 37, 'Three rows of the teaching clustering score below 0 on the silhouette. How do they relate to the write-up''s line on where the clustering fails?', '["All three are core shaly-sand, the facies that line names", "The 9 rows the line says fall with sandstone", "Limestone rows, which the line leaves out", "They sit in cluster 1, the mixed cluster the line names"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Rows 12, 135 and 169 are shaly-sand in cluster 3, and the write-up''s line reads "shaly-sand: 9 of 47 rows fall with sandstone". Those 9 rows sit in cluster 1; the three below 0 sit in cluster 3, so they are other rows of the same facies.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 38, 'The write-up prints the elbow and silhouette figures beside its choice of k 4. Where do those figures point on the cored rows?', '["Both peak at k 4, confirming the choice the core made", "The engine picks k 4 from them and prints them as its reason", "The largest drop fraction and the best silhouette both sit at k 3", "To k 4, which bestSilhouetteK names as best"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Step 1 of the workflow returns drop fractions 0.668972, 0.282430, 0.112303 at k 3, 4, 5 and the best silhouette at k 3. The core describes 4 facies and the write-up takes k 4, with that evidence printed beside it. No elbow is picked automatically, and bestSilhouetteK is 3.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 39, 'Two labellings of the cored rows score an adjusted Rand index of 1.000000 against each other. What follows?', '["Each of them agrees perfectly with the core facies", "They share the same cluster number on every row", "They were produced by one seed and one start", "They are the same grouping, whatever their cluster numbers"]'::jsonb, 3, 'An index of 1.000000 marks the same grouping: seed 1 against seed 3 scores 1.000000 while no row keeps its number and the seeds differ. Against the core each still scores 0.872413.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 40, 'The Ward tree is cut at k 5 (52, 22, 22, 54, 30) and then at k 6 (52, 22, 8, 14, 54, 30). Which cluster splits?', '["The 52-row cluster, which gives up 8 and 14 of its rows", "One of the two 22-row clusters, into 8 and 14", "The 54-row cluster, into 8 and 46", "A split of the 30-row cluster into 8 and 22 at the next undoing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Going from k 5 to k 6 undoes merge 174, at 2.814872, which had joined clusters of 8 and 14 into 22. Every other cluster keeps its size, 52, 22, 54 and 30, because the tree nests and one step down undoes one merge.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 41, 'Across the elbow table with its silhouette column, which k scores the lowest mean silhouette, and what drop fraction sits beside it?', '["k 7: silhouette 0.492984 and the lowest fraction, 0.056380", "k 5, silhouette 0.503776, with a drop fraction of 0.112303", "k 8: silhouette 0.266816, drop fraction 0.068791", "k 4, at 0.545063 and 0.282430"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The elbow with its silhouette reads 0.266816 at k 8, the lowest of k 2 to 8, where the drop fraction is 0.068791. k 7 has the lowest drop fraction, 0.056380, and k 4 and k 5 score higher silhouettes.'),
  ('facies', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 42, 'Which reading of the teaching clustering''s report ties each imperfect figure to one cell of the contingency table?', '["Sandstone precision 0.847458 and shaly-sand recall 0.808511 both come from the 9 shaly-sand rows of cluster 1", "Sandstone recall 0.847458 and shaly-sand precision 0.808511 both come from the 9 rows of cluster 3", "Shale precision 0.894118 comes from the 29 shale rows of cluster 0 alone", "Limestone F1 0.917431 comes from the 54 limestone rows of cluster 2"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The 9 shaly-sand rows of cluster 1 are predicted sandstone, lowering sandstone''s precision to 0.847458 and shaly-sand''s recall to 0.808511. Sandstone''s recall and shaly-sand''s precision are 1.000000; 0.894118 and 0.917431 are the F1 of shaly-sand and sandstone; limestone and shale score 1.000000.');
